{
  "schema_version": "howl.exploration_result/v1",
  "exploration_id": "hd-20261009-212457-b02b8f798c1e",
  "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p2-critique",
  "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (coaching-hire evaluation, non-tender decisions, qualifying offers, NPB/KBO posting bids, postseason roster construction, arbitration filing). For EACH: (1) does the decision recur and how large is the value at stake per year for one club; (2) is the decision-relevant data lawfully public and available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) how many historical decisions exist for a retrospective test against the club's simple baseline, and is the outcome observable; (4) actionability; (5) what sophisticated clubs and public sources already do; (6) what would make it LOOK useful while giving little or no advantage, including small samples and confounding. Note that each candidate's attached assumptions are pooled from a larger batch; judge each on its own IDEA text. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis across the set. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, and IDEA only for a sharpened replacement.",
  "originating_component": "operator",
  "authority": {
    "type": "ADVISORY",
    "executable": false
  },
  "candidates": [
    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212457-b02b8f798c1e/candidates/0/0",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212457-b02b8f798c1e",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p2-critique",
      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (coaching-hire evaluation, non-tender decisions, qualifying offers, NPB/KBO posting bids, postseason roster construction, arbitration filing). For EACH: (1) does the decision recur and how large is the value at stake per year for one club; (2) is the decision-relevant data lawfully public and available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) how many historical decisions exist for a retrospective test against the club's simple baseline, and is the outcome observable; (4) actionability; (5) what sophisticated clubs and public sources already do; (6) what would make it LOOK useful while giving little or no advantage, including small samples and confounding. Note that each candidate's attached assumptions are pooled from a larger batch; judge each on its own IDEA text. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis across the set. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, and IDEA only for a sharpened replacement.",
      "text": "UNKNOWN: supplied_facts_for_any_claim (the evidence list holds only the objective text, with no data, counts, dollar values or Cubs records)\nCITE: objective_context\nASSUMPTION: Every critique below is judgment from general baseball-operations structure. None is verified, and no figure is quoted because none was supplied.\nASSUMPTION: The attached assumptions are pooled across many ideas, and many (rain delays, stolen bases, draft pools, ticketing) are irrelevant to these six. Only those matching each IDEA text are used.\n\nASSUMPTION: [1 Coaching hire] The decision recurs only a few times a year, and each hire is a single, unrepeatable choice. The value at stake is unquantified, and the ledger's sign is unknown.\nASSUMPTION: [1] Public staff data is not point-in-time clean. Titles, assistant and minor-league roles and start dates in press releases and media guides are incomplete and revised after the fact.\nASSUMPTION: [1] Few players move organizations alongside a given coach. Movers are selected (acquired because the club expects improvement) and coach credit is shared among several staff. Playing time, health and the player's own regression to the mean are further confounders.\nASSUMPTION: [1] A retrospective test is possible if ratings built through year T predict player change under the same coach in T+1 better than a tenure-and-reputation proxy. The number of coaches with enough movers may be too small for intervals narrow enough to change a hire.\nASSUMPTION: [1] Outcome observability is weak because the counterfactual is missing. Clubs with private tracking and biomechanics data probably evaluate coaches better than any public panel can.\nASSUMPTION: [1] It would look useful by ranking coaches whose past-year gains are mostly regression to the mean. The ranking would look reasonable with no persistence, so year-over-year persistence is the key falsification test.\n\nASSUMPTION: [2 Non-tender] This recurs annually with a hard early-December deadline (unverified; the CBA may change it). The value at stake is each eligible player's projected arbitration salary minus replacement cost, which is real but unquantified.\nASSUMPTION: [2] Salary projections are public-ish and available at decision time. The replacement market (who will be non-tendered league-wide, and at what price) is not known when the Cubs decide, so the forecaster must predict other clubs' choices.\nASSUMPTION: [2] A backtest has many player-years, but price and next-season output are observed only for players who signed. Players who retired, went abroad or signed minor-league deals create survivorship bias.\nASSUMPTION: [2] The baseline (tender if WAR per dollar beats a threshold) is the same quantity the forecaster uses, so gains may be only a threshold re-tune. The prior finding that a simple rule captures most of the value in the low-attention pool suggests a ceiling here too.\nASSUMPTION: [2] It would look useful from post-hoc selection of bargain non-tendered players, where single-season WAR noise is large and winners are remembered. Sophisticated clubs already carry internal projection and arbitration models.\n\nASSUMPTION: [3 Qualifying offer] The decision is annual but applies only to the Cubs' own pending free agents who meet eligibility rules. The Cubs' eligible list is unknown, so expected value may be near zero in many years.\nASSUMPTION: [3] The QO amount, acceptances and signings are public, but the number of cases per year is small. The accepted and declined classes are lopsided, so calibration is weak and one executive's judgment may match it.\nASSUMPTION: [3] The idea's baseline (\"offer when market AAV clearly exceeds the QO\") already needs a market AAV estimate. The model replaces that estimate, so the claimed edge reduces to forecast accuracy on a handful of players.\nASSUMPTION: [3] The compensation-pick value depends on CBA terms that may change at expiration (unverified). Counterfactual value cannot be observed, and acceptance is driven by player preference, health and agent strategy that public signals miss.\nASSUMPTION: [3] It would look useful through rare, memorable accepted-QO cases and high accuracy on obvious calls such as a star who is declined.\n\nASSUMPTION: [4 NPB/KBO posting] This is a handful of events per year across the league, and the Cubs only matter when they pursue a given player. Per-signing value is plausibly large but unquantified.\nASSUMPTION: [4] The posting window is largely public. Ownership of NPB pitch-tracking and detailed batted-ball data is limited, and what is public may lag decision time.\nASSUMPTION: [4] Previously posted players with MLB outcomes are few, so the translation model has high variance and heterogeneous eras and league environments. Outcomes are observable but censored, because players who were not signed leave no MLB data.\nASSUMPTION: [4] The winner's curse means a more accurate public model still does not give an edge unless the Cubs bid below the consensus. Clubs with international scouting, video and private data are likely ahead, and the idea overlaps minor-league translation work.\nASSUMPTION: [4] It would look useful by fitting the few posted stars in hindsight, with overfit coefficients and nothing out of sample.\n\nASSUMPTION: [5 Postseason roster] The decision recurs only in years the Cubs qualify, one to four times a year by the candidate's own text. Expected annual value is discounted by qualification probability (unknown).\nASSUMPTION: [5] Rosters, opposing starters and platoon splits are public at the decision time. Injury status at the roster deadline may be incompletely public.\nASSUMPTION: [5] Pooled league-wide postseason rosters give a modest number of series, but each series outcome is dominated by noise. A series-win-probability difference from the 25th and 26th roster spots may be too small to detect with any realistic sample.\nASSUMPTION: [5] Alternatives are not observed, so the backtest is a simulation scored by its own model and can embed the biases it tests. Clubs likely already run internal series simulators.\nASSUMPTION: [5] It would look useful by retrofitting a few memorable pinch-runner or reliever moments in a winning run, which is outcome bias.\n\nASSUMPTION: [6 Arbitration filing] This recurs every January across the arbitration-eligible roster, and awards compound into later raises, so it is among the more regular decisions. The value is capped by the gap between the two filed numbers, since the panel picks one.\nASSUMPTION: [6] Filed numbers, results and the comparable-player rules are largely public. The exact arguments and the panel's reasoning are not, and hearings are rare because most cases settle. Settlements are not hearing outcomes, so the training labels come from a selected subset.\nASSUMPTION: [6] The count of historical hearings may be too small and shifts with CBA rule changes (expiration date and new rules unverified). Whether to settle or go to hearing is a strategic choice also tied to club policy and player relations, not just EV.\nASSUMPTION: [6] The baseline (midpoint settlement) is a hearing-free outcome, so the EV comparison needs an assumed hearing probability and a model of player willingness. Specialized staff and outside counsel probably already hold this knowledge.\nASSUMPTION: [6] It would look useful through a predictor tuned to hearing outcomes in which the filing midpoint alone already predicts the winner, and through ignoring that club-won and player-won cases are selected by who chose to go to hearing.\n\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Dollar and win value at stake is unquantified for all six. Rank the candidates by value times decision count times the maximum plausible edge before building anything.\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Sample size is the shared weakness (QO, posting, postseason, hearings, coach movers). Pooling league-wide raises n but strips Cubs-specific context, and no tool can beat a simple baseline with only a few dozen noisy labels.\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Many of these decisions are driven by counterparties (agents, player preference, rival bidders, the league). A club-side prediction model does not control the outcome, and the rules may change at the CBA expiration (unverified).\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Dated snapshots are a precondition. Any backtest must freeze the data as of the decision day (projections, injury status, staff titles), or hindsight leaks into every candidate.\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Testing many candidates and many specifications without a pre-registered baseline comparison produces false positives. Any claimed edge needs out-of-sample results against the simple rule.\nASSUMPTION: [Blind spot] Non-tender, QO, arbitration and posting all draw on one payroll and 40-man budget, so evaluating them separately misses interactions. Private Cubs practice is unknown, and public-data parity with sophisticated clubs is plausible for every candidate.\nUNKNOWN: cubs_private_models_and_practices\nUNKNOWN: cubs_qo_eligible_players_2026\nUNKNOWN: annual_hearing_count_sample_size\nUNKNOWN: count_of_posted_players_with_mlb_outcomes\nUNKNOWN: detectable_coach_effect_size\nUNKNOWN: post_2026_cba_arbitration_rules\nUNKNOWN: measured_win_probability_value_of_marginal_postseason_slot\n\nIDEA: Build a single pre-registered, dated-snapshot backtest harness for the offseason decisions (non-tender, QO, arbitration, posting). It would freeze public inputs as of each decision date, score each simple club baseline against a candidate model out of sample, and report n and interval width, so any candidate whose interval includes zero edge is dropped before software is built.\nIDEA: Replace the coaching-ledger build with a persistence-first test: check on public data whether apparent coach effects in year T repeat in T+1 for coaches who moved clubs. Proceed to a hiring tool only if they persist beyond regression to the mean, and treat a null result as the useful output.\nIDEA: Sharpen arbitration to settle-versus-hearing selection: estimate, from public filed-number gaps and past outcomes, the filing-gap threshold above which hearings favor the club. State the result as a decision table with explicit uncertainty rather than a point predictor.",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
