    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212457-b02b8f798c1e/candidates/0/3",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212457-b02b8f798c1e",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p2-critique",
      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (coaching-hire evaluation, non-tender decisions, qualifying offers, NPB/KBO posting bids, postseason roster construction, arbitration filing). For EACH: (1) does the decision recur and how large is the value at stake per year for one club; (2) is the decision-relevant data lawfully public and available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) how many historical decisions exist for a retrospective test against the club's simple baseline, and is the outcome observable; (4) actionability; (5) what sophisticated clubs and public sources already do; (6) what would make it LOOK useful while giving little or no advantage, including small samples and confounding. Note that each candidate's attached assumptions are pooled from a larger batch; judge each on its own IDEA text. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis across the set. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, and IDEA only for a sharpened replacement.",
      "text": "IDEA: Replace the six-candidate portfolio with one narrow, falsifiable pilot: a dated-snapshot backtest of tender/non-tender and qualifying-offer decisions pooled league-wide, scored against the \"projected WAR per dollar vs club threshold\" and \"offer if market AAV clearly exceeds QO\" baselines, with every input timestamped to the decision date.\nASSUMPTION: The supplied evidence contains no facts, so every size, count, and rate below is unverified; no statistic is asserted here.\nUNKNOWN: cubs_private_models_and_practices\nUNKNOWN: supplied_evidence_for_any_claim\n\nIDEA: Candidate 1, coaching-hire evaluation. Keep only as a null-result study, not a decision tool: a coach-movement panel with matched non-moving controls and a pre-registered test of year-over-year persistence of coach effects.\nASSUMPTION: Recurrence is real but small (a handful of hires per offseason), and the value per hire is unknown; \"large and recurring\" is a hypothesis, not evidence.\nASSUMPTION: Staff tenure records (assistant and minor-league roles) are incomplete or inconsistently dated, which makes the linkage to players the weakest point.\nASSUMPTION: Confounds include selection (clubs hire coaches after improvements), regression to the mean, roster turnover, playing time, park, injury, and organizational infrastructure; a coach touching few players gives wide intervals.\nASSUMPTION: Outcomes are observable only with a lag and with heavy noise; a hindsight-free test must build ratings on seasons up to T and predict T+1 against a reputation proxy.\nASSUMPTION: Clubs likely already run private attribution, and public sources (Statcast leaderboards, media reporting) give partial versions.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful through anecdotal success stories and in-sample rankings that vanish out of sample.\nUNKNOWN: detectable_coach_effect_size\nUNKNOWN: public_coach_tenure_dataset_completeness\n\nIDEA: Candidate 2, non-tender decisions. This is the strongest candidate: it recurs every year, the decision date is fixed, and tender decisions, projected salaries, and later signings are public, so a pooled backtest across all clubs is possible.\nASSUMPTION: Value per club is plausibly material only for a few borderline players a year; the number of those is unknown.\nASSUMPTION: Projected arbitration salaries are public only as third-party estimates, whose vintage as of the deadline must be preserved to avoid hindsight.\nASSUMPTION: The outcome is only partially observable: you see what non-tendered players did elsewhere, but not what the tendered player would have done, so the counterfactual is asymmetric.\nASSUMPTION: Clubs already apply cost-per-WAR thresholds and public projections (e.g., projection systems, public salary estimators), so the edge over the baseline may be zero.\nASSUMPTION: Replacement-market forecasting depends on who is available only after the deadline, so the forecast may be no better than a generic replacement-level price.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful by showing that non-tendered players sometimes produce well; that is survivorship, and the correct test compares against tendered peers at equal price.\nUNKNOWN: historical_nontender_signing_outcomes_dataset\n\nIDEA: Candidate 3, qualifying offers. Treat as a small-n calibration exercise embedded in the tender pilot, not a standalone product.\nASSUMPTION: The decision applies to only a few players in a given year and only when the club has eligible free agents, and the number of historical acceptances is small, so calibration is weak.\nASSUMPTION: Compensation value depends on CBA terms, which must be verified, and which may change at the next agreement.\nASSUMPTION: Acceptance depends on player preference and the market, which are not public at the decision time.\nASSUMPTION: Experienced executives and agents already use \"market AAV vs QO\" heuristics, and acceptance has historically been rare, so a rule of \"offer when market exceeds the QO\" is difficult to beat.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful through a high accuracy score driven by the base rate of declined offers.\nUNKNOWN: current_qo_amount_2026\nUNKNOWN: historical_qo_acceptance_count_by_year\nUNKNOWN: cubs_qo_eligible_players_2026\n\nIDEA: Candidate 4, NPB/KBO posting bids. Use as a Bayesian translation with wide intervals, reporting where the model cannot separate itself from simple ratios plus scouting.\nASSUMPTION: Events are rare (a few relevant players per winter), so the value per signing may be large, but the annual aggregate for one club is small.\nASSUMPTION: Calibration cases are few, and the league environments (ball, rules, park) change, so out-of-sample error likely dominates.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-tracking from foreign leagues is partially public at best, and scouting video is private to clubs.\nASSUMPTION: Prices come from competitive bidding and posting rules, so a more accurate forecast must still overcome the winner's curse and the cap/fee structure.\nASSUMPTION: The outcome is observable (MLB production), but only for those who were posted and signed, which creates selection bias.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful by fitting a handful of famous players well.\nUNKNOWN: count_of_posted_players_with_mlb_outcomes\nUNKNOWN: public_availability_of_npb_kbo_pitch_tracking\n\nIDEA: Candidate 5, postseason roster construction. Reframe as a one-time sensitivity analysis to bound the value of the 25th and 26th roster slots, and build nothing until that bound exceeds measurement noise.\nASSUMPTION: The decision recurs only when the club qualifies (zero to four times a year), so expected value per year is discounted by qualification probability.\nASSUMPTION: Marginal slots may see little playing time, and series-win-probability differences are likely small relative to noise.\nASSUMPTION: A retrospective test has few rounds per club and relies on league-wide pooling, which removes health and context; counterfactual rosters are not observed.\nASSUMPTION: Postseason rosters and transaction logs are public, but injury status at the deadline may not be.\nASSUMPTION: Sophisticated clubs already simulate this privately, and public playoff-odds tools cover the seeding part.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful through vivid after-the-fact stories (a pinch runner who scored), which reflect hindsight.\nUNKNOWN: measured_win_probability_value_of_marginal_postseason_slot\nUNKNOWN: public_postseason_roster_archive_completeness\n\nIDEA: Candidate 6, arbitration filing. Restrict to a settle-vs-hearing expected-value calculator built on public hearing results, and report the sample size before any modeling.\nASSUMPTION: The decision recurs every January across the eligible roster, so volume is the highest of the six, but contested hearings are few league-wide each year and typically few or none for one club.\nASSUMPTION: Many clubs follow a file-and-trial policy that is itself known to agents, so the strategy is a policy choice, not a prediction problem.\nASSUMPTION: Panels decide on a restricted set of criteria and comparables, so the filing midpoint plus comparables are strong baselines; the public record of the arguments and the outcomes may be too thin to beat them.\nASSUMPTION: The outcome is observable (a win or loss at the hearing), but settlements before the hearing hide the counterfactual, and selection into hearings biases the sample.\nASSUMPTION: Specialized staff and outside counsel already hold this knowledge, so the clubs are probably ahead.\nASSUMPTION: It will look useful through high in-sample fit with few observations and many predictors.\nUNKNOWN: annual_hearing_count_sample_size\nUNKNOWN: post_2026_cba_arbitration_rules\n\nIDEA: Blind spot analysis across the set: add a shared evaluation protocol, with decision-date snapshots, a pooled league-wide test, a pre-registered simple baseline, and a reported minimum detectable effect, before any build decision.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 1 is that five of six candidates are rare, small-n decisions, so none is likely to show a statistically detectable edge in a retrospective test.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 2 is that the club-specific value is dominated by private information (medical, makeup, player preference, negotiation positions) that public data cannot capture.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 3 is that the CBA expiration and rule changes may invalidate the historical records; the dates and the terms are unverified.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 4 is that all six are one-off decisions, and the evidence suggests that recurring, high-volume, in-season decisions were not weighed at equal depth.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 5 is that the candidate assumptions are pooled from a larger batch, so many (draft, ticketing, rain delays) do not apply, and counting them as evidence would inflate the criticism.\nASSUMPTION: Blind spot 6 is that none of the six identifies the cost of building and maintaining the software against a likely modest gain, or whether the front office would act on a recommendation.\nCONFLICT: none_identified_no_evidence_supplied",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
