      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "The supplied evidence contains no facts, so every size, count, and rate below is unverified; no statistic is asserted here.",
        "Recurrence is real but small (a handful of hires per offseason), and the value per hire is unknown; \"large and recurring\" is a hypothesis, not evidence.",
        "Staff tenure records (assistant and minor-league roles) are incomplete or inconsistently dated, which makes the linkage to players the weakest point.",
        "Confounds include selection (clubs hire coaches after improvements), regression to the mean, roster turnover, playing time, park, injury, and organizational infrastructure; a coach touching few players gives wide intervals.",
        "Outcomes are observable only with a lag and with heavy noise; a hindsight-free test must build ratings on seasons up to T and predict T+1 against a reputation proxy.",
        "Clubs likely already run private attribution, and public sources (Statcast leaderboards, media reporting) give partial versions.",
        "It will look useful through anecdotal success stories and in-sample rankings that vanish out of sample.",
        "Value per club is plausibly material only for a few borderline players a year; the number of those is unknown.",
        "Projected arbitration salaries are public only as third-party estimates, whose vintage as of the deadline must be preserved to avoid hindsight.",
        "The outcome is only partially observable: you see what non-tendered players did elsewhere, but not what the tendered player would have done, so the counterfactual is asymmetric.",
        "Clubs already apply cost-per-WAR thresholds and public projections (e.g., projection systems, public salary estimators), so the edge over the baseline may be zero.",
        "Replacement-market forecasting depends on who is available only after the deadline, so the forecast may be no better than a generic replacement-level price.",
        "It will look useful by showing that non-tendered players sometimes produce well; that is survivorship, and the correct test compares against tendered peers at equal price.",
        "The decision applies to only a few players in a given year and only when the club has eligible free agents, and the number of historical acceptances is small, so calibration is weak.",
        "Compensation value depends on CBA terms, which must be verified, and which may change at the next agreement.",
        "Acceptance depends on player preference and the market, which are not public at the decision time.",
        "Experienced executives and agents already use \"market AAV vs QO\" heuristics, and acceptance has historically been rare, so a rule of \"offer when market exceeds the QO\" is difficult to beat.",
        "It will look useful through a high accuracy score driven by the base rate of declined offers.",
        "Events are rare (a few relevant players per winter), so the value per signing may be large, but the annual aggregate for one club is small.",
        "Calibration cases are few, and the league environments (ball, rules, park) change, so out-of-sample error likely dominates.",
        "Pitch-tracking from foreign leagues is partially public at best, and scouting video is private to clubs.",
        "Prices come from competitive bidding and posting rules, so a more accurate forecast must still overcome the winner's curse and the cap/fee structure.",
        "The outcome is observable (MLB production), but only for those who were posted and signed, which creates selection bias.",
        "It will look useful by fitting a handful of famous players well.",
        "The decision recurs only when the club qualifies (zero to four times a year), so expected value per year is discounted by qualification probability.",
        "Marginal slots may see little playing time, and series-win-probability differences are likely small relative to noise.",
        "A retrospective test has few rounds per club and relies on league-wide pooling, which removes health and context; counterfactual rosters are not observed.",
        "Postseason rosters and transaction logs are public, but injury status at the deadline may not be.",
        "Sophisticated clubs already simulate this privately, and public playoff-odds tools cover the seeding part.",
        "It will look useful through vivid after-the-fact stories (a pinch runner who scored), which reflect hindsight.",
        "The decision recurs every January across the eligible roster, so volume is the highest of the six, but contested hearings are few league-wide each year and typically few or none for one club.",
        "Many clubs follow a file-and-trial policy that is itself known to agents, so the strategy is a policy choice, not a prediction problem.",
        "Panels decide on a restricted set of criteria and comparables, so the filing midpoint plus comparables are strong baselines; the public record of the arguments and the outcomes may be too thin to beat them.",
        "The outcome is observable (a win or loss at the hearing), but settlements before the hearing hide the counterfactual, and selection into hearings biases the sample.",
        "Specialized staff and outside counsel already hold this knowledge, so the clubs are probably ahead.",
        "It will look useful through high in-sample fit with few observations and many predictors.",
        "Blind spot 1 is that five of six candidates are rare, small-n decisions, so none is likely to show a statistically detectable edge in a retrospective test.",
        "Blind spot 2 is that the club-specific value is dominated by private information (medical, makeup, player preference, negotiation positions) that public data cannot capture.",
        "Blind spot 3 is that the CBA expiration and rule changes may invalidate the historical records; the dates and the terms are unverified.",
        "Blind spot 4 is that all six are one-off decisions, and the evidence suggests that recurring, high-volume, in-season decisions were not weighed at equal depth.",
        "Blind spot 5 is that the candidate assumptions are pooled from a larger batch, so many (draft, ticketing, rain delays) do not apply, and counting them as evidence would inflate the criticism.",
        "Blind spot 6 is that none of the six identifies the cost of building and maintaining the software against a likely modest gain, or whether the front office would act on a recommendation."
      ],
